\begin{abstract}
This paper develops an integrated framework to forecast Ecuador’s Food Consumer Price Index (CPI-Food) and to diagnose the subgroups that exert the greatest inflationary pressure, combining deep learning with nonlinear regression and incidence analysis. Using official monthly series from the National Institute of Statistics and Census (INEC) for CPI-Food and eleven COICOP food subindices spanning January 2006 to October 2025, the study first models the aggregate index as a multivariate time series characterized by strong trends, seasonality, shocks, and marked nonstationarity. A multi-step Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network is specified to predict horizons of 3–6 months from sliding temporal windows, with standardized inputs and a single output layer that jointly estimates future values. On the test set, the LSTM achieves low error levels (RMSE = 1.18, MAPE = 1.01\%), closely reproducing the level, slope, and seasonal behavior of CPI-Food and maintaining stable accuracy as the forecast horizon lengthens, while the largest residuals are concentrated around periods of exogenous shocks. To quantify the internal structure of food inflation, the second stage constructs a Random Forest model on monthly variation rates of CPI-Food and its subindices, revealing that a small subset of components—especially bread and cereals, non-alcoholic beverages, meat, and the general CPI—accounts for most of the explained variance, whereas more volatile categories such as fruits, vegetables, fish, and oils and fats contribute more marginally in aggregate terms. The joint use of LSTM for forecasting and Random Forest for incidence analysis yields a data-driven early warning scheme that can support public institutions and private agents in anticipating short-term food inflation, prioritizing critical subgroups, and designing more proactive food security and price-stabilization policies. 
\end{abstract}

