\section{Review of related work}
The recent literature on economic forecasting and price-index prediction shows a clear shift from traditional linear models toward machine-learning and recurrent-neural-network approaches, which directly shapes the methodological decisions of this project. Works such as \textit{Sibai et al. (2024)} \cite{b1} compare MLP, KNN and Random Forest for Consumer Price Index (CPI) prediction, evidencing that nonlinear models substantially reduce MSE, RMSE and MAE, although their generalization deteriorates beyond 24-month horizons—an insight that supports our focus on short-term forecasting for the Canasta Familiar Básica (CFB). Likewise, \textit{Torres et al. (2020)} \cite{b2} highlight the strength of RNNs in monthly price series, reaching accuracies above 94\% for the Brazilian basic basket and capturing abrupt market shifts, a relevant property given the historical volatility of Ecuador’s CFB and episodes of imported inflation. Research centered on Ecuador, such as \textit{Riofrío (2024)} \cite{b3}, confirms strong seasonality and nonlinear relations between household income and basket cost, where SVR and MLP achieve low MAPE, reinforcing the need to evaluate hybrid and nonlinear models. In addition, \textit{Yang and Guo (2021)} \cite{b4} show that deep architectures like LSTM and GRU outperform ARIMA and Bayesian models across MSE, SMAPE and MAPE by capturing long-range temporal dependencies, justifying their inclusion as key candidates in our pipeline. Studies specifically on Ecuadorian CPI, such as \textit{Riofrío et al. (2020)} \cite{b5}, further indicate that LSTM and SVR excel for macroeconomic forecasts under 12 months, supporting the idea that Ecuadorian dynamics align better with nonlinear models—patterns that extend to the CFB. Consistently, the TICEC 2023 study by \textit{Riofrío, Infante and Hernández} \cite{b6} introduces modern techniques such as FFT, 4Theta and the Darts library, reporting low MAE and strong robustness for Ecuadorian monthly series from 2001–2023, guiding us toward methods that integrate decomposition, smoothing and weak periodic patterns, features also present in the CFB. Finally, \textit{Nannapaneni and Kulkarni (2023)} \cite{b7} emphasize hybrid ensembles combining neural networks with linear components like ARIMA or SARIMA for nonlinear and chaotic series, achieving strong short-term performance. Altogether, this body of work not only supports our model selection but also establishes methodological principles: using short horizons, prioritizing nonlinear and hybrid models, and applying rigorous metrics such as MSE, MAE, MAPE and SMAPE to obtain reliable forecasts of Ecuador’s Canasta Familiar Básica.


\section{Identification of the problem}
The CPI for Food (Consumer Price Index) is the official indicator that summarizes monthly food price behavior and directly affects the cost of the Basic Family Basket. Studying its dynamics is of great importance for social and economic planning, especially for low-income Ecuadorian households and social protection programs. 
Food inflation in Ecuador shows episodes of high volatility, which are associated with external and internal conflicts such as supply chain disruptions, agricultural supply shocks, variations in international prices, and extreme weather events.
This translates into abrupt and non-linear behaviors that are not well captured or anticipated by traditional monitoring models. The problem is also exacerbated by the need to disaggregate the aggregate index, as some subgroups may have a disproportionate impact on total food inflation.
It is therefore important to develop a model that improves short-term forecasting and can identify and quantify specific sources of pressure.



 The cost of food is directly related to food security and poverty, where an unexpected increase can result in the vulnerability of thousands of families below the poverty line. Knowing this prediction is of utmost importance for entities such as MIES or NGOs that have social programs, mitigating the impact before the crisis materializes. Identifying these pressure points is extremely useful for the Ministry of Agriculture or local governments in designing targeted support policies such as temporary subsidies, logistical improvements, or market regulation. On the other hand, for logistics chains and supermarkets, it helps with inventory planning and negotiations with suppliers. Anticipation reduces risk and volatility in the supply chain.

\section{Problem statement}
The problem with predicting the CPI food price is time series modeling, which is characterized by monthly data with marked nonlinear seasonality. Traditional statistical methods such as ARIMA fail to capture these complex and abrupt dynamic relationships caused by the aforementioned internal and external events.

Based on this behavior, the main algorithm to be used for short-term forecasting (3 to 6 months) is LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory). This is a neural network specialized in remembering information with the ability to identify how past events have or may have an effect on the current context. Unlike other models that forget past events and fail to capture socioeconomic complexity.

Adopting an approach based on artificial intelligence and machine learning is justified by the literature review and the improved predictive and diagnostic capabilities that traditional models lack. ML-based models are better at modeling the nonlinear relationships and complex patterns that define food inflation. Due to the nature of the project, algorithms such as Random Forest are also required to identify the drivers of food CPI inflation. By using the prices of food subgroups, it is possible to accurately determine which products have the greatest weight or influence.

\subsection{Objectives}
In this paper, the overall objective is to develop a short-term prediction model based on machine learning for the CPI value in the food sector, in an explainable and quantifiable manner, as well as to identify the product subgroups that drive this variation, with the aim of providing technical inputs for proper planning in the country's food security.

To achieve the project design, the following specific objectives are proposed.

Process the monthly historical series of the CPI-Food and its sub-indices, by breaking them down into trends, seasonality, and shock inspection, for the LSTM neural network training process.

Identify the influence of food sub-indices on the variation of the CPI-Food by implementing regression algorithms such as Random Forest in order to establish an accurate diagnosis of the items that generate the greatest inflationary pressure.

Evaluate the prediction accuracy of the 3- to 6-month LSTM model using absolute and quadratic error metrics (MSE, MAE, and RMSE) to validate its usefulness as an economic forecasting tool.

\section{Justification and application of the methodology to be used}
\subsection{Data collection}
The data necessary for the study was collected from the National Institute of Statistics and Census (INEC), which is the official body responsible for constructing the CPI in Ecuador. The data was obtained directly from its institutional statistical repository, corresponding to time series covering the period from January 2006 to October 2025, which guarantees historical depth for the predictive model.

The first set corresponds to the CPI-Food, which is the main series that acts as the dependent variable or target for the LSTM-based forecasting model.

The second set corresponds to the price sub-indices of eleven food subgroups according to the national COICOP classification (Classification of Individual Consumption by Purpose), which is a United Nations standard system for classifying and analyzing consumer spending. These are used as independent variables in the Random Forest regression model and in the SHAP attribution analysis to identify the items with the highest inflationary pressure.

The official INEC documentation guarantees methodological consistency, objectivity, and national representativeness, which is essential to ensure the validity of the results and conclusions in economic analysis and in the design of public policies related to food security.

\subsection{Data cleaning, preprocessing and transformation.}

To assure the quality of the suggested model, it is necessary to clean, preprocess, and transform the data.

\subsection{Loading the databases}
The databases from INEC were loaded, one file containing the CPI for the food group and another file containing the price indices for food products at the class level. This separation is due to the way in which the institution publishes the information. This is the starting point for the objective of integrating the two into a single dataset built around the temporal dimension.

\subsection{Initial Transformations}
The date information was normalized. The months were in Spanish text format, so we mapped the names of the months to numerical values. Some grammatical errors were found and corrected. Then, a variable named date was constructed in datetime format using the first day of each month as a convention. This procedure is justified in order to chronologically order the observations, generate time indices, and apply time series techniques. It also allows us to establish a time key that will later be used as a reference to merge both data sets correctly.

\subsection{Food CPI}
In this dataset, the first step is to filter the indicators, retaining only those observations where the indicator type corresponds to “Index.” This is because the original file also includes percentage variations and other derivatives that are not suitable for training the LSTM network. Next, only the columns corresponding to the date and the index value were retained. For better management, the latter was renamed ipc alimentos index.


\subsection{Food subgroups}
The product dataset underwent a more thorough cleaning process. As a first step, rows with the indicator “Index” and the disaggregation level “Class” were filtered, representing the most disaggregated level of the COICOP consumption classification available in the database. This methodological action corresponds to the interest in capturing information on food groups at the highest possible level of detail, which is an important requirement for identifying sources of inflationary pressure at the subcategory level.

The variable names were standardized, as the original columns included special characters. The fields were renamed with lowercase letters, $snake_case$, and quotation marks, periods, and commas were removed. Then, only the columns for date, product class description, and index value, which are essential for the analysis, were retained. The format was also changed from long to wide using a table where each row represents a month and each column represents the price index for each food category. This restructuring is justified by the type of model to be used. Algorithms such as LSTM, Random Forest, and SHAP require matrices where each row (time step) considers all variables simultaneously.

These enhancements assure code reproducibility, minimize coding difficulties, and provide compatibility with varied execution situations.


\subsection{Integration of the two sets}
The dataset used is a monthly multivariate time series covering January 2006 to October 2025 with 238 observations, where each row represents a month and each column corresponds to a specific component of food inflation.
All variables are from Ecuador's INEC and are expressed as price indices normalized to the official CPI base year. These monthly indices measure the evolution over time of the general price level that households must pay for a representative basket of consumer goods and services.
Each sub-index reflects, by means of an index number, the weighted average level of prices relative to historical average expenditure. Using the INEC's current methodology with base year 2014 = 100, all index values are compared to this level. Values higher than this base indicate that prices are higher than those of the base year, while lower values indicate lower prices.
The nature of this data allows for the analysis of inflation, changes in purchasing power, and the relative incidence of food subgroups over time, but it has the limitation of not being able to directly estimate the cost in dollars in any physical unit.


\subsection{Identification and description of variables}
The dataset used is a monthly multivariate time series covering January 2006 to October 2025 with 238 observations. All variables are from Ecuador's INEC and are expressed as price indices normalized to the official CPI base year.

