\subsection{Statistical and Temporal Nature of the Variables}

Firstly, both the \texttt{ipc\_food\_index} and the product sub-indices show a clear long-term upward trend. The aggregate index rose from approximately 60.91 in January 2006 (first recorded data) to 122.25 in October 2025 (last recorded data), representing an increase of approximately 100\%.


This pattern is consistent with the increase of food inflation over two decades and illustrates that the series are not stationary.

The graphs of the series and the analysis of their components indicate the presence of trends, seasonality, and irregular components. In categories such as fruits, vegetables, and fish, behavior based on agricultural and fishing cycles can be observed. On the other hand, more processed items such as bread and cereals or sugar, jam, honey, chocolate, and sugar sweets have smoother trajectories, dominated by long-term trends and cost adjustments.

In terms of variability, standard deviations allow us to distinguish products whose inflationary dynamics do not strictly follow the general index. Subgroups such as \texttt{oils and fats}, \texttt{mineral waters, soft drinks, fruit and vegetable juices}, \texttt{coffee, tea, and cocoa}, and \texttt{food products n.e.c.} show deviations of more than 20 points from the index, evidencing high volatility and sensitivity to external events. On the other hand, categories such as meat, \texttt{milk\_cheese\_and\_eggs} and \texttt{sugar\_jam\_honey\_chocolate\_and\_sugar\_sweets} show more moderate variations, as a result of more regulated markets or more stable supply chains.

This analysis is relevant to the project's objectives, as it allows us to identify subgroups that could act as sources of inflationary pressure on the \texttt{CPI\_food\_index}.


Another key effect is the occurrence of extreme events.  Peaks in fruits (140.20), vegetables (137.87), and oils and fats (157.75) are correlated with global food price shocks or crises like the COVID-19 pandemic.  These values might be considered as high-impact observations from the standpoint of economic forecasting, and as such, they ought to be incorporated into the model to strengthen its reaction to inflationary stress scenarios.



Finally, the nature of the variables requires key decisions such as transforming the data so that it behaves more stably. It is also useful to adjust all variables to similar scales so that the model learns without bias or associated problems. Finally, it is important to organize the information into ordered time sequences, since each value depends on the previous ones. These decisions provide the necessary basis for designing the model and meeting the objective of predicting and understanding food inflation.


