\subsection{Random Forest Model}

By focusing on explaining how monthly variations in the CPI-Food index arise from changes in the sub-indices, the Random Forest model offers a complementary perspective.  This non linear approach, when there are drastic changes in certain groups, allows for more accurate calculations than the linear model. This indicates the presence of threshold effects and complex links between variables that require non linear separations to be represented correctly.

When it comes to understanding monthly inflation, oils and fats, fruits, vegetables and legumes, and unidentified food items typically rank among the most important variables.

Different groups contribute differently to fluctuations in the CPI-Food index. Some groups show monthly variations that have a greater weight in the overall index. On the other hand, there are groups such as meat and dairy products with a lower standard deviation and more uniform trajectories.

