\section{Implications}

The results of the study have obvious consequences for both the analysis of food inflation and its operational management. First, the LSTM's strong performance indicates that the CPI-Food demonstrates sufficiently consistent temporal patterns to allow for trustworthy short-term forecasts.  This makes it possible for public entities to foresee situations and take preventative action instead of just responding.

Secondly, the incidence analysis using the Random Forest model shows that the variation in the index is concentrated in a few high-impact items. On the other hand, there are groups that have more local or seasonal effects. This asymmetry indicates that macroeconomic surveillance should focus on the most important sub-products, but without neglecting the social relevance of fresh items with less statistical weight.

Finally, the integration of LSTM for forecasting and Random Forest for incidence structure forms the basis of an early warning system. This framework can help institutions such as MIES, sectoral ministries, and local governments to plan support policies, public procurement, and logistical interventions further in advance, and allows the private sector to optimize inventories, prices, and critical inputs in the face of expected variations in food inflation.


\subsection{Conclutions}
The results suggest that LSTM models can estimate short-term food inflation with excellent accuracy.  The model accurately replicates the CPI-Food's steady temporal trends its recent track, giving a mathematical foundation for anticipating its evolution over three and six months and significantly surpassing linear techniques.

The CPI-Food's dynamics are largely focused in a small number of subcomponents, according to the variable significance analysis.  Items such as bread and cereals, non-alcoholic beverages, and meat comprise the structural core of the index, while other groups have a more limited or seasonal impact.  This makes it easy to determine the items whose price growth is most crucial for collective behavior.

The integration of LSTM as a forecasting model and Random Forest as a structural analysis tool demonstrates that it is possible to identify sources of inflationary pressure in advance. The combination of short-term projections and incidence maps by subcategory forms the basis of an early warning system that facilitates planning, prevention, and the formulation of more proactive food security policies.


\subsection{Recomendations}
It is recommended to institutionalize a monthly CPI-Food forecasting system based on regular upgrades of the LSTM model with recent INEC data.  To assist with budget management and public program planning, this system should produce estimates for the next three and six months along with confidence intervals.

It is recommended that the subgroups with the highest prevalence within the index be monitored first, specifically meat, bread and cereals, and non-alcoholic drinks.  By creating specialized monitoring dashboards, price fluctuations and related risk variables might be systematically observed, focusing analytical attention on the items with the biggest structural influence.

It is proposed that the outputs of the LSTM and Random Forest be integrated into the decision-making processes of social, agricultural, and supply institutions.   Preventive changes to food subsidies, government procurement, logistical agreements, or interventions in important supply chains might be possible if inflationary pressures were anticipated.

